\section{Where Single-Signal Selection Fails}\label{sec:selection_gap}

\paragraph{Definitional vs.\ empirical.}
We decompose the correlation-selection gap into two layers.
The first is structural: four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates from a given input (\S\ref{sec:formulation}), so they estimate quality across inputs but cannot rank candidates within an instance---selection failure is a design property, not an empirical finding.
The second layer is empirical and constitutes the core diagnostic: LP---the only per-sample signal---suffers compression under constrained decoding, with 60\% of SciERC instances showing within-instance LP range below 0.041 nats (Appendix~\ref{app:logprob_ties}), effectively eliminating the variation needed for sample discrimination. Even where LP varies, selection gains depend on LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy alone.
Cross-instance $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}) is attenuated by varying instance difficulty; within-instance $\rho$ directly measures whether LP discriminates candidates for a fixed input, making it the relevant predictor of selection success.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$; 3-seed Oracle${=}0.781$, Greedy${=}0.667$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. $^\dagger$One score per input.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
\midrule
Random       & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{Correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Bars: QE $\rho$; line: $\Delta$F1 vs.\ greedy (3-seed mean). Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy (all $p{\geq}0.29$). Dashed green: oracle headroom ($+11.4$\,pp; oracle headroom $=$ best-of-$N$ F1 $-$ greedy F1); dotted gray: random.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}); the oracle shows 11.4\,pp headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$\,pp ($p{=}0.29$), below MDE (${\approx}$1.5\,pp; $n{=}529$; Table~\ref{tab:power_analysis}).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.39$\,pp ($p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.021$, selection family $k{=}21$) at low degeneracy (13.0\% vs.\ 12.2--67.9\%), while structural signals still degrade (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic cascade.}
We identify a cascade rather than independent factors:
(1)~instance degeneracy sets the ceiling on possible improvement (SciERC 12.2\%, CoNLL 61.7\%);
(2)~within non-degenerate instances, oracle headroom bounds the opportunity (CoNLL ${\approx}4$\,pp vs.\ SciERC 11.4\,pp);
(3)~LP compression limits exploitation of that opportunity (Few-NERD within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$ vs.\ SciERC $\rho{\approx}0.26$).
We did not find evidence that degeneracy alone predicts LP gain across datasets ($\rho{=}0.052$, $n{=}23$, $p{=}0.81$; post-hoc power: 80\% power requires $n{\approx}85$ for $\rho{=}0.3$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though degeneracy co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
The cascade stages interact rather than combining into a predictive model ($R^2{<}0$); each is supported by controlled ablations (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}).
On non-degenerate instances, selection alignment $\alpha$ approaches zero ($0.000$--$0.161$) despite moderate quality correlations ($\rho$ up to 0.47)---this near-zero alignment characterizes where the correlation-selection gap emerges (\S\ref{sec:theory}).

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed (${\approx}$65\% F1).
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 51\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
$N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}; $N{=}16$ for scaling (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). AUROC binarizes at per-dataset median F1. Best in \textbf{bold}. RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}; $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & .684 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .692 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .629 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & \textbf{.749} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC, structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ at $N{=}16$, but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered, 3-seed means). $p$: paired bootstrap.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
WNUT-17 & 1{,}287 & .452 & 67.9\% & $-$.446 & $-$0.45\,pp & .72 \\
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 61.7\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .667 & 12.2\% & .159 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.0\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy ($+1.39$~pp, $p{<}0.001$; structural signals degrade: VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp), with LP 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp), generalizing to LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}). Training convergence modulates degeneracy (5-epoch: $31$\%, halving LP gains; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

Signal ranking is task-specific (EM dominates SciERC, SJ leads Few-NERD); per-type analysis shows MV degradation concentrates in low-F1 types ($\rho{=}0.90$, $p{=}0.002$) and 7/8 types reverse MV direction between pretrained and finetuned decoding (Appendix~\ref{app:pertype}), ruling out type-specific factors.

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction.}
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across 23 configurations ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; $n{=}23$, insufficient for reliable correlation---we treat this as supplementary evidence; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Structural signals remain negative regardless of sample budget; while the selection gap narrows with increasing $N$ (MV $\Delta$ improves from $-2.71$\,pp at $N{=}8$ to $-1.65$\,pp at $N{=}16$; Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}), diminishing returns suggest closing the gap requires impractical sample sizes.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}\label{sec:robustness_main}

Signal rankings are robust across temperatures, seeds ($W{=}1.0$), and $N$ (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\paragraph{Scale and controlled ablations.}
Qwen3-4B QE is comparable to 8B ($|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$; Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation_app}), yet selection remains below greedy.
LoRA rank${=}8$ lowers degeneracy to 7.2\% but LP stays below greedy, confirming both conditions are necessary; supervised classifiers and hidden-state probes also fail (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}, \ref{app:hidden_probe}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}\label{sec:prescriptive}

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
We aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}). In zero-shot, LP-weighting is necessary (SciERC $+1.5$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

LP-weighted construction improves on Few-NERD ($+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.006$, $k{=}10$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}); SciERC shows a smaller, non-significant gain ($+0.32$\,pp, 3-seed); CoNLL shows no gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with high degeneracy.
Gains require both low degeneracy \textit{and} sufficient oracle headroom (Few-NERD: $D{=}13$\%, headroom${=}12.4$\,pp).\footnote{Holm--Bonferroni \cite{holm1979simple} within families ($k{=}10$, $21$, $9$); global $k{=}57$: $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$ (Appendix~\ref{app:holm}).}

\paragraph{Complementary strategies.}
Construction and LP selection are complementary, associated with within-instance LP alignment $\rho_{\text{adj}}$ (Few-NERD $0.516$ favors both; SciERC $0.167$ favors construction only).
QE signals also support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, filtering yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}

